Abstract

Augmented reality (AR) systems are becoming common tools in industrial workplaces. However, factory workers are still concerned about whether head-mounted display (HMD)-based AR systems distract their awareness of the environment and therefore pose safety risks. The purpose of this study was to assess users' experience of real-world awareness when using an AR system. 19 study participants played a wooden block logic game in a laboratory with three different setups: real, AR and virtual reality (VR). Based on this study, it can be concluded that HMD-based AR systems do not decrease users’ awareness of their surroundings if the virtual content is minimal and the task is done while seated. However, it was seen that more research in this area with more interactive virtual content is required. This study is an important step in understanding how AR may affect future work in industrial and safety-critical environments.
Introduction

Augmented reality (AR) technologies are becoming mature enough to be adopted in everyday use. Pierdicca et al. (2017) see AR as one pillar of new industrial environments. However, there are still challenges to overcome when implementing AR systems in industry (Masood and Egger, 2019; Palmarini et al., 2018). For example, employees are concerned about the use of head-mounted display (HMD)-based AR systems because they may distract them (Aromaa and Kaasinen, 2018; Sunwook et al., 2016). They are afraid that the AR systems could decrease workers' awareness of the surrounding environment. The concern is valid in the sense that, for example, the use of the Pokémon Go game has led to accidents. Barbieri et al. (2017) point out that these mobile games could pose a significant risk to users if they play while walking, cycling, or driving, because the users become distracted and may ignore other traffic and surrounding hazards. In addition, Kim and Gabbard (2019) found out that AR head-up displays (HUDs) can direct the vehicle driver's attention away from critical road elements (e.g., other vehicles or road signs). However, they conclude that the AR content can be informative or distractive based on the selected perceptual forms of graphical elements.

By definition, AR allows the user to see the real world, with virtual objects superimposed upon or composited with the real world (Azuma et al., 2001). The main application areas of AR technologies are in design, maintenance and assembly (Nee et al., 2012). Nee et al. (2012) categorize AR-assisted systems into three groups based on the information presentation method: presenting information on HMD (see examples Helin et al., 2018; Henderson and Feiner, 2009; Makris et al., 2016), presenting information with a tablet or smartphone (see examples Aaltonen et al., 2016; Aromaa and Väänänen, 2016; Polvi et al., 2018; Sánchez et al., 2016), and projecting information at the workplace (see examples Doshi et al., 2017; Funk et al., 2017; Korn et al., 2013). In this introduction, also some studies related to smart glasses and HUDs are addressed even though they are not always actual AR because the information is merely “glued” on the display to the view. In actual AR systems, the digital information is presented as if it exists in reality.

Attention, awareness and cognitive load are issues that are important to consider when using AR systems on a factory floor because they can have an effect on safety. When people are disrupted by divided attention, it is possible that they experience inattentional blindness. Inattentive blindness results from a lack of attention that is not associated with vision defects or deficits, as an individual fails to perceive an unexpected stimulus in plain sight (e.g. the famous gorilla study by Simons and Chabris, 1999). Situation awareness (SA) can be defined as a perception of elements in the environment within a volume of time and space, the comprehension of their meaning and the projection of their future status (Endsley, 1988). In other words, SA means that people are aware of the specific information that is needed to perform a task. Context awareness is similar to SA, but in context awareness users are aware of all relevant information a specific environment can provide to them.

Some AR studies have proven that AR systems, smart glasses and HUDs can have a positive effect on users' context awareness, safety and/or decrease cognitive load. For example, when increasing context awareness users can be provided with information otherwise not seen in their environment: Schwarz and Fastenmeier (2018) studied AR use when providing warnings about obstructed hazards and Grubert et al. (2017) discussed sensing a user's current context. Park and Park (2019) agree in their review that automotive HUDs can often improve driving safety and enhance the driver's SA. Borgmann et al. (2017) studied the feasibility, safety and usefulness of Google Glass for urological surgery. It was seen that smart glass-assisted urological surgery is both feasible and safe and additionally provides several useful functions for urological surgeons. Kim and Dey (2009) compared two car navigation systems and one of them was an AR system. They found that the AR system was useful, especially for older drivers. In addition, the drivers using the AR display system had significantly fewer navigation errors and divided attention-related issues. According to Kim et al. (2019) having graphic-based information constantly visible on the HMDs led to increased job performance and reduced perceived workload as compared to the use of text-based information, or the use of a traditional paper pick list. Schwarz and Fastenmeier (2018) conclude that for warning situations while driving a car well designed AR provide the opportunity to increase the amount of information conveyance without distracting the driver. Rowen et al. (2019) studied Google Glasses when navigating marine vessels. They agreed that the use of wearable AR displays could improve operator performance and situation awareness.

There are also studies that indicate that AR systems may increase the cognitive load. According to Gross et al. (2018), the cognitive workload on forklift drivers increases significantly when using display information devices such as HMDs. Wang et al. (2019) study shows that users experience more workload when using Google Glass-based AR instructions in disassembling a smartphone than printed manual. Baumeister et al. (2017) evaluated cognitive load of three AR display technologies: projector-based display (spatial AR), Microsoft HoloLens and Samsung Gear VR with monitor instructions. They discovered that a limited field of view could introduce increased cognitive load requirements. The findings suggest that some of the inherent restrictions of HMDs (e.g., field of view, resolution, vergence, accommodation) materialize as increased user cognitive load. Kerr et al. (2012) found that divided attention between the AR display and the environment brought up some safety concerns. At least four (out of nine) participants failed to notice they were approaching a road junction, and had to be warned of the approaching traffic. Kim et al. (2019) agrees that walking while processing visual information presented via HMDs may have important workplace safety implications. Kruijff et al. (2010) indicated that perceptual problems that may occur in AR can derive from issues in the environment (e.g. visibility), capturing (e.g. colour contrast), augmentation (e.g. occlusion), display device (e.g. field of view) and/or the user (e.g. individual differences). According to Aaltonen and Laarni (2017), smart glasses can be problematic from the aspects of comfort and distraction. For example, participants said that the smart glasses force the users to divide their attention among multiple things.

How much AR systems distract users and make them unaware of what is happening in their environment seems not to have been studied widely. Therefore, there is a need to study especially the user experience of HMD-based AR systems more thoroughly. According to Kim et al. (2019) limited information is available regarding the effective use of head-worn displays in workplaces. In addition, the studies are dispersed in the field of science, and are related to several different kinds of tasks (e.g. driving, surgery, assembly). There is considerable variation in available technologies: many studies consider head-worn displays (monocular, binocular) and not the actual AR HMDs where virtual objects are presented as a part of a real environment.

Our study addresses the gap in the research concerning how much HMD-based AR systems distract users. The goal of the study was to investigate how users experience their real-world awareness while using an AR system. The study was conducted in a laboratory and the participants performed the same tasks with three different systems (real, AR and virtual reality (VR)) on the reality-virtuality (RV) continuum (Fig. 1). The RV continuum is a continuous scale categorising displays from the completely real to the completely virtual (Milgram et al., 1995). By studying three different reality-virtuality level systems it is possible to understand how users experience the AR system compared to real and virtual systems. Kalawsky (1993) defines VR as a technology which provides human beings with the means of manipulation and sensory modalities in a virtual environment. Practically, it means that humans are able to navigate in the virtual environment (e.g. move from one place to another), manipulate objects (e.g. pick up a virtual object), and get sensory feedback (e.g. see and hear things).

This paper raises the important research topic of how distracting AR systems can be. The results of this study can be used when studying the suitability of HMD-based AR systems for industry use. In addition, the study highlights the need for improved evaluation methods for studying users with AR systems. The structure of the paper is as follows: Section 2 presents material and methods, Section 3 describes results of the study, Section 4 discusses the findings and proposes directions for future research, and Section 5 summarizes research conclusions.
